[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-kotoba-resource-protocol", :doc/title "Kotoba Resource Protocol", :doc/path "90-docs/protocols/kotoba-resource-protocol.edn", :doc/doc_type "doc", :doc/source-format "edn", :doc/body "# Kotoba Resource Protocol\n\nStatus: design baseline 0.1 (2026-07-14); 0.2 addendum 2026-07-17 (§16); 0.3 addendum 2026-08-14 (§17, content URI has no path)\n\nKRP is an EDN-first addressing protocol for giving one logical resource consistent identities\nacross the Web, AT Protocol, IPLD/IPFS, Git, Datomic/DataScript, filesystems, and local CLJ/CLJS\nruntimes. It is a mapping contract, not another storage network and not a replacement URI scheme.\n\n## 1. Problem\n\nSystems repeatedly overload one string called `path` with incompatible meanings:\n\n- a human-facing mutable name;\n- a network location;\n- an owner or security authority;\n- a database identity;\n- an immutable content version;\n- a path inside a file or DAG;\n- a UI view or command.\n\nThis produces collisions such as `/{org}/{repo}` meaning both a project and a game, query strings\ncarrying resource identity, fake SHA strings presented as CIDs, or filesystem layout becoming a\npublic API. KRP separates these dimensions and makes their relationship explicit data.\n\n## 2. Core model\n\nThe canonical KRP address is an EDN map. It has up to six orthogonal coordinates:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/authority  \"did:web:gftd.example\" ; who controls the mutable name\n :kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"       ; human-facing ownership boundary\n :kotoba/collection :network.isekai/asset   ; typed record family\n :kotoba/key        \"rifle\"                 ; opaque key within the collection\n :kotoba/revision   {:cid \"bafy...\"}        ; optional immutable version\n :kotoba/selector   [:meshes 0 :material]}  ; optional traversal inside that version\n```\n\nThe identity tuple is:\n\n```clojure\n[:kotoba/address authority repository collection key revision selector]\n```\n\nCoordinates MUST NOT be inferred from display text, author strings, filesystem paths, or one\nanother. Missing coordinates have meaning:\n\n- repository only: mutable repository root;\n- repository + collection: collection view;\n- repository + collection + key: mutable named record;\n- revision present: immutable snapshot;\n- selector present: a value reached inside the selected snapshot.\n\n## 3. The four identities of a resource\n\nEvery resource may expose four distinct identifiers. They are related, never interchangeable.\n\n### 3.1 Name\n\nA stable, human-facing alias controlled by an authority:\n\n```text\nhttps://isekai.network/gftd/nightglass/assets/rifle\n```\n\nNames are mutable: the owner may publish a new version under the same name. A rename creates an\nalias/redirect; it does not silently reuse the old name for an unrelated resource.\n\n### 3.2 Record\n\nAn owner-scoped protocol record:\n\n```text\nat://did:web:gftd.example/network.isekai.asset/rifle\n```\n\nAn AT URI identifies `(DID, collection, rkey)`. It is authoritative and mutable, but not content-\naddressed. KRP uses DID authorities for durable output; handles are presentation aliases only.\n\n### 3.3 Content\n\nAn immutable content address:\n\n```text\nipfs://bafy...\nhttps://ipfs.kotobase.net/ipfs/bafy...\n```\n\nA CID identifies encoded content or a DAG root. A checksum such as `sha256-deadbeef`, a database\nID, a Git ref, and a KRP key are not CIDs. A CID identifies encoded content. The public URI is `ipfs://{cidv1}` with no path\n(ADR-2608145100). Traversal lives in `:kotoba/selector` (EDN), not in the URI.\nUnixFS directory paths after a CID are not KRP content identity.\n\n### 3.4 Location\n\nA transport-specific place from which bytes can currently be retrieved:\n\n```text\nhttps://cdn.example/rifle.glb\ns3://bucket/objects/...\nfile:///cache/...\n```\n\nLocations may disappear or change without changing logical identity. Integrity is established by\nthe revision CID/digest, not by trusting the location.\n\n## 4. HTTP projection\n\nThe universal public pattern is:\n\n```text\n/{org}/{repo}[/{collection-route}[/{key}]]\n```\n\nRules:\n\n1. `/{org}/{repo}` is the repository's primary resource. It MUST NOT be wrapped in a redundant\n   platform collection. A game repository is `/gftd/nightglass`, not\n   `/gftd/network-isekai/games/nightglass`.\n2. Child collections are used only for resources actually owned by that repository:\n   `/gftd/nightglass/assets/rifle`, `/gftd/nightglass/forks/k123`.\n3. Collection routes are declared projections of qualified collection IDs. `assets` may project\n   `:network.isekai/asset`; code never derives one from the other by pluralization.\n4. Keys are opaque. Clients MUST NOT parse timestamps, types, parents, or authorization from keys.\n5. Query parameters select representation, filters, pagination, or ephemeral UI state. They MUST\n   NOT carry the primary identity of a resource.\n6. Fragments are client-side selectors and MUST NOT be treated as server identity.\n7. Commands do not masquerade as resources. UI actions use forms; machine commands use versioned\n   API or RPC namespaces such as `/api/v1/...` or AT Protocol XRPC NSIDs.\n8. Unknown resources return 404 and never fall through to a homepage.\n\n### 4.1 Host-scoped form (2026-08-05)\n\nAn authority MAY move the org coordinate from the path into the DNS label:\n\n```text\nhttps://{org}.{authority-domain}/{repo}[/{collection-route}[/{key}]]\n```\n\nThis is the same address; only the projection differs. `{org}` is still the ownership\nboundary and `{repo}` is still the primary resource — rules 1-8 apply unchanged, with\nthe first path segment read as `{repo}` instead of `{org}`.\n\nThree properties motivate it, and only the first is cosmetic:\n\na. **The org acquires a surface.** A path prefix `/{org}/` is served by nobody, so a\n   platform that projects both coordinates into the path violates rule 8 at the\n   one-segment level unless it separately implements an org route. A host root is the\n   org resource by construction.\nb. **Each org receives a distinct web origin.** §6.1 already prefers the subdomain form\n   for browser-executable content for exactly this reason; that reasoning is not specific\n   to CIDs and applies to any published repository that ships script.\nc. It matches how the reference implementation of this idea (GitHub Pages,\n   `{org}.github.io/{repo}`) is deployed.\n\nConstraints an implementation MUST observe:\n\n1. **Only declared hosts resolve.** A hostname that parses as `{label}.{domain}` does not\n   imply the org exists — the same discipline §3 states for locations. Undeclared org\n   hosts MUST NOT be served.\n2. **The org host serves that org only.** It MUST NOT mirror the platform's own surfaces\n   (marketplace, API, sign-in), or one resource acquires two canonical URLs.\n3. **Certificate validation paths stay reachable.** `/.well-known/` MUST pass through, or\n   new org hosts can never be provisioned.\n4. **Migration is declared, not inferred.** While both forms are live, the path form is an\n   alias that redirects (301) to the host form. An implementation MUST NOT decide this\n   from a runtime probe (DNS, certificate state); it is a declaration in the registry, so\n   that the projection is reproducible wherever it is computed.\n5. **A subdomain does not partition cookies.** Unless `{authority-domain}` is on the Public\n   Suffix List, `Domain={authority-domain}` cookies are shared across every org host. An\n   authority publishing third-party content MUST either use host-only cookies or publish\n   from a separate registrable domain. **Adding the authority's own domain to the PSL is\n   not the fix** — it would also forbid the authority's apex from setting cookies at all,\n   which is why GitHub moved Pages to a separate domain rather than listing github.com.\n\nThe path form remains the baseline. An authority that has not declared org hosts continues\nto serve `/{org}/{repo}` unchanged.\n\nDeployed: `itonami.cloud` (ADR-2608057000). `murakumo.cloud` (ADR-2607299300) still declares\nthe path form and has not implemented either.\n\nExample repository kinds:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba.repository/kind :game}          ; root plays\n{:kotoba.repository/kind :asset-library} ; root browses\n{:kotoba.repository/kind :service}       ; root describes service\n{:kotoba.repository/kind :project}       ; root presents project\n```\n\nThe kind changes representation, not identity or path shape.\n\n## 5. AT Protocol projection\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/authority \"did:web:gftd.example\"\n :kotoba/collection :network.isekai/asset\n :kotoba/key \"rifle\"}\n;; => at://did:web:gftd.example/network.isekai.asset/rifle\n```\n\n- Qualified keyword namespace/name projects to an NSID through an explicit registry.\n- The registry MUST reject collisions; keyword string conversion alone is not sufficient.\n- Record keys follow the Lexicon's declared rkey type and AT Protocol normalization.\n- The record stores the KRP repository name, canonical HTTP URL, current CID strong reference,\n  media/blob references, schema version, and previous record/CID when lineage matters.\n- The AT repository is an identity repository owned by a DID. It is not assumed to be the same\n  concept as a Git or product repository.\n\nSuggested record envelope:\n\n```clojure\n{:$type \"network.isekai.asset\"\n :kotobaRepository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n :key \"rifle\"\n :canonical \"https://isekai.network/gftd/nightglass/assets/rifle\"\n :content {:cid \"bafy...\" :codec \"dag-cbor\"}\n :media [{:cid \"bafk...\" :mediaType \"model/gltf-binary\"}]\n :schemaVersion 1}\n```\n\n## 6. IPLD/IPFS projection\n\nKRP manifests SHOULD be encoded canonically as DAG-CBOR or another registered IPLD codec. Links\nto immutable dependencies are native CID links, not URL strings when the codec supports links.\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/type :network.isekai/asset\n :kotoba/key \"rifle\"\n :asset/model {:kotoba/link {:cid \"bafk...\"}}\n :asset/materials [{:kotoba/link {:cid \"bafy...\"}}]}\n```\n\nThe CLJ/EDN in-memory representation uses tagged values or explicit `:kotoba/link` maps; the codec\nadapter converts them to IPLD Links. Traversal is kept separate:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/revision {:cid \"bafy-root\"}\n :kotoba/selector [:asset/materials 0]}\n```\n\nGateway projection follows `/ipfs/{cid}[/{path}]`. Mutable publication may additionally use IPNS,\nbut the KRP repository name does not become an IPNS name automatically.\n\n### 6.1 DID, CID, and resource binding\n\nKRP assigns one responsibility to each identifier:\n\n| Identifier | Meaning | Mutability |\n|---|---|---|\n| DID | authority that may publish or rotate a resource head | key/service state may change |\n| KRP address / AT URI | stable logical resource name under that authority | head may change |\n| CID | exact encoded content or DAG root | immutable |\n| HTTP/IPFS URL | retrieval or presentation location | may change |\n\nResolution is a three-stage chain, not a string-concatenation rule:\n\n```text\nDID URL / KRP address\n  -> DID document service endpoint\n  -> signed resource-head record\n  -> CID\n  -> verified IPLD/IPFS content\n```\n\nThe DID document SHOULD publish one resolver service, rather than one entry per asset:\n\n```json\n{\"id\": \"did:web:gftd.example#kotoba\",\n \"type\": \"KotobaResourceResolver\",\n \"serviceEndpoint\": \"https://gftd.example/.well-known/kotoba\"}\n```\n\nA DID URL may select that service and a relative logical resource using DID URL dereferencing:\n\n```text\ndid:web:gftd.example?service=kotoba&relativeRef=/gftd/nightglass/assets/rifle\n```\n\nThe service returns a signed head; it does not make the DID URL content-addressed:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba.head/address {:kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n                       :kotoba/collection :network.isekai/asset\n                       :kotoba/key \"rifle\"}\n :kotoba.head/authority \"did:web:gftd.example\"\n :kotoba.head/cid \"bafy...\"\n :kotoba.head/previous \"bafy-old...\"\n :kotoba.head/sequence 42\n :kotoba.head/issued-at #inst \"2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\"\n :kotoba.head/proof {...}}\n```\n\n`https://{did-web-domain}/{cid}` MUST NOT be assumed to have protocol meaning. It collapses\nauthority, HTTP origin, mutable resolution, and immutable content into one ambiguous path and\ncollides with human repository routes. An HTTP binding MUST use an explicit form:\n\n```text\nhttps://gftd.example/.well-known/kotoba/cid/{cid}  authority verification/location\nhttps://gftd.example/ipfs/{cid}                   IPFS path gateway, if implemented\nhttps://{cid}.ipfs.gftd.example/                  isolated IPFS subdomain origin\nipfs://{cid}                                      transport-neutral content address\n```\n\nThe subdomain form is preferred for browser-executable content because each CID receives an\nisolated web origin. Bare `/{cid}` MAY be a documented redirect, but MUST NOT be emitted as a\ncanonical KRP identifier or used as proof of DID ownership.\n\n## 7. Datomic/DataScript projection\n\nLogical identity and immutable revisions are separate entities:\n\n```clojure\n{:db/ident :kotoba.resource/address\n :db/valueType :db.type/tuple\n :db/tupleTypes [:db.type/string :db.type/keyword :db.type/string]\n :db/unique :db.unique/identity}\n\n{:kotoba.resource/address [\"gftd/nightglass\" :network.isekai/asset \"rifle\"]\n :kotoba.resource/authority \"did:web:gftd.example\"\n :kotoba.resource/head [:kotoba.cid/value \"bafy...\"]}\n\n{:kotoba.cid/value \"bafy...\"\n :kotoba.revision/codec :dag-cbor\n :kotoba.revision/bytes 12345}\n```\n\n- Use lookup refs for logical records.\n- Store CID as a unique value on revision entities.\n- Datomic entity IDs never leave the database as public identifiers.\n- `as-of` database time and CID revision are different axes; both may be recorded.\n- Retractions change the current index, not immutable content already published by CID.\n\n## 8. Git and filesystem projection\n\nGit repositories map naturally to `:kotoba/repository`, but Git commit IDs remain source revisions,\nnot content CIDs unless a manifest explicitly binds them.\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n :kotoba/source {:git/url \"https://github.com/gftd/nightglass\"\n                 :git/commit \"abc123...\"\n                 :git/path \"assets/rifle.edn\"}\n :kotoba/revision {:cid \"bafy...\"}}\n```\n\nFilesystem paths are local locations. Moving a checkout does not rename the resource. Public routes\nMUST be generated from manifests, never inferred from workspace directory depth.\n\n## 9. Canonical EDN\n\nKRP data is ordinary EDN with these requirements:\n\n- maps are unordered semantically; canonical encoding sorts map keys by their encoded bytes;\n- sets MUST be converted to deterministically sorted vectors before content hashing;\n- keywords used on the wire MUST be qualified, except closed local enums;\n- instants use UTC; ratios, symbols, and arbitrary tagged literals require registered codecs;\n- absence uses omitted keys, not a mixture of omitted and `nil`, in hashed manifests;\n- strings are Unicode NFC; routing slugs are lowercase ASCII;\n- hashing applies to canonical encoded bytes, never `pr-str` unless that exact profile is declared;\n- schema/version is part of hashed data.\n\nMinimal manifest:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/schema :kotoba.resource/manifest-v1\n :kotoba/address\n {:kotoba/authority \"did:web:gftd.example\"\n  :kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n  :kotoba/collection :network.isekai/asset\n  :kotoba/key \"rifle\"}\n :kotoba/content {:cid \"bafy...\" :codec :dag-cbor}\n :kotoba/links []}\n```\n\n## 10. Resolution\n\nA resolver accepts a KRP address and returns an evidence-bearing result:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba.resolve/address {...}\n :kotoba.resolve/record {:at-uri \"at://...\" :cid \"bafy-record\"}\n :kotoba.resolve/content {:cid \"bafy...\" :verified? true}\n :kotoba.resolve/locations [\"https://...\" \"ipfs://...\"]\n :kotoba.resolve/provenance [{:kind :atproto/commit :cid \"...\"}\n                             {:kind :git/commit :id \"...\"}]}\n```\n\nResolution order:\n\n1. validate and normalize the address;\n2. resolve authority (DID document/policy);\n3. resolve mutable record/name to a head CID;\n4. fetch from one or more locations;\n5. verify bytes against CID/digest;\n6. traverse selector under codec rules;\n7. return value plus evidence, never a bare unverified value.\n\nResolvers are protocols in CLJ:\n\n```clojure\n(defprotocol IKotobaResolver\n  (resolve-name [this address opts])\n  (fetch-content [this revision opts])\n  (verify-content [this revision bytes opts]))\n```\n\nNetwork, Datomic, IPFS, Git, and filesystem behavior is supplied by adapters. Pure address parsing,\nnormalization, projection, and validation remain `.cljc`.\n\n### 10.1 Mutable-head mechanisms considered\n\nAll mechanisms below may point to a CID, but they are not interchangeable:\n\n| Mechanism | Mutable pointer | Strength | Constraint | KRP role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| IPNS | IPNS key -> CID | distributed, signed, IPFS-native | DHT latency, republishing, expiry | repository-head mirror |\n| DNSLink | DNS name -> CID/IPNS | human-readable, existing DNS operations | DNS control and TTL | organization/domain mirror |\n| AT Protocol record | DID + collection + rkey -> CID | owner, history, signed repository | requires PDS/AppView | asset/game metadata record |\n| Kotobase signed head | Datomic record -> CID | query, ACL, history, transaction | service availability | primary application index |\n| Radicle RID + canonical refs | RID/ref -> Git OID -> CID | local-first, delegates, signed refs | Git OID/CID mapping | repository governance |\n| Signed Git ref/tag | ref/tag -> commit -> CID | existing development tooling | forge/source oriented | source release provenance |\n| OCI tag + digest | tag -> digest/CID | registry, large artifact, promotion | digest/CID mapping | WASM, GLB, CAR distribution |\n| Signed HTTP head manifest | URL -> signed EDN -> CID | fast, CDN-friendly, simple | depends on web origin | browser fast path |\n| DID document service | DID service -> resolver | authority discovery and key policy | avoid per-resource DID updates | resolver discovery |\n| Append-only IPLD head log | head CID -> previous CID | audit, fork detection, offline merge | needs separate head discovery | history and replication |\n\nThe default composition is DID service discovery, an AT/Kotobase signed logical head, optional\nIPNS and DNSLink mirrors, and CID-addressed content. Radicle is complementary: its repository\nidentity and signed canonical references are useful for source governance, but a Radicle source\nhead is not the canonical identity of every runtime asset.\n\nAn IPNS name MAY be recorded in the signed head and SHOULD resolve to the same CID during its\ndeclared validity window. Clients treat disagreement as evidence to report, not as permission to\nsilently choose the newest-looking value.\n\nENS `contenthash` is an optional eleventh adapter for Ethereum environments. Chain cost and\nEthereum dependency keep it outside the KRP baseline.\n\n### 10.2 Recommended resolution profile\n\nThe normal fast path and fallbacks are:\n\n```text\nHTTP signed manifest / Kotobase\n  -> AT Protocol record\n  -> IPNS or DNSLink\n  -> CID fetch from local blockstore or trustless gateway\n  -> CID verification\n```\n\nFallback order is availability policy, not authority precedence. Every successful path MUST return\nthe signed evidence it used and converge on CID verification. A client MUST NOT accept bytes merely\nbecause they came from the canonical HTTP domain.\n\nKRP RECOMMENDS one IPNS key per repository, recorded alongside the other projections:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n :kotoba/head {:cid \"bafy-current-manifest\" :sequence 42}\n :kotoba/names\n {:http \"https://isekai.network/gftd/nightglass\"\n  :at \"at://did:web:gftd.example/network.isekai.repository/nightglass\"\n  :ipns \"ipns://k51...\"\n  :dnslink \"nightglass.gftd.example\"}}\n```\n\nAn IPNS adapter validates the record signature, sequence, TTL, and validity before following its\n`/ipfs/{cid}` value. Operators must republish records before expiry and monitor DHT resolution\nlatency. Cached records MUST NOT be served beyond their validity merely because the CID content\nremains available.\n\n## 11. Mutation and publication\n\nPublication is an ordered state transition:\n\n```text\ncanonical EDN → encode → CID → store/pin → signed record → mutable name/index\n```\n\nThe name/index MUST NOT point at a CID until content storage succeeds. Updating the name creates a\nnew signed record revision; it never mutates content at the previous CID. Multi-resource publication\nuses a root manifest CID so readers observe one atomic head.\n\nThe standard repository publication workflow is:\n\n1. normalize canonical EDN and encode it as DAG-CBOR;\n2. store asset blobs and calculate their CIDs;\n3. construct, encode, and store the immutable root manifest;\n4. confirm block/CAR durability or pinning;\n5. transactionally update the Kotobase signed head;\n6. update the AT Protocol record;\n7. publish the repository IPNS record;\n8. update DNSLink when its operational policy requires it.\n\nSteps 5-8 are retryable projections of already durable content. IPNS or DNS publication failure\nMUST NOT roll back a committed primary head, but it MUST be reported as mirror lag. Failure before\ncontent durability MUST prevent every mutable-head update. Retries carry the same target CID and\nidempotency/publication identifier.\n\nFor multi-authority repositories, a Radicle-inspired policy MAY derive a canonical head from\nindependent signed proposals:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/repository-id \"krp:...\"\n :kotoba/identity-cid \"bafy...\"\n :kotoba/delegates [{:did \"did:key:...a\" :weight 1}\n                    {:did \"did:key:...b\" :weight 1}]\n :kotoba/threshold 2\n :kotoba/heads [{:did \"did:key:...a\" :cid \"bafy-head\" :signature \"...\"}\n                {:did \"did:key:...b\" :cid \"bafy-head\" :signature \"...\"}]\n :kotoba/canonical-head \"bafy-head\"}\n```\n\nThis adopts stable repository identity, delegate policy, signed peer heads, threshold selection,\nand append-only collaborative objects. It does not equate a Radicle RID, Git OID, or peer location\nwith an IPLD CID.\n\n## 12. Security and normalization\n\n- Decode a URL segment once; reject encoded slash/backslash, control characters, empty segments,\n  `.`, `..`, and noncanonical percent encoding.\n- Keep authority in signed data; never derive authorization from an HTTP org slug alone.\n- Resolve DID to keys and service endpoints under explicit cache/rotation policy.\n- Treat rkeys, CIDs, selectors, media types, codecs, and remote locations as hostile input.\n- Apply SSRF policy before fetching locations; CID verification does not make fetching safe.\n- Path gateways must isolate executable origins; KRP does not weaken IPFS gateway origin rules.\n- Aliases are explicit signed edges with expiry/policy, not implicit fuzzy matching.\n\n## 13. Compatibility invariants\n\nEvery implementation MUST preserve:\n\n1. Same normalized address map means the same logical resource.\n2. Same valid CID means the same encoded content.\n3. Same AT URI means the same owner-scoped record, not necessarily the same content.\n4. HTTP URLs are aliases/views; redirects do not rewrite historical CIDs.\n5. Database IDs, Git commits, filesystem paths, checksums, CIDs, and record keys are typed and never\n   substituted based only on string shape.\n6. A repository root is never redundantly nested under its own resource kind.\n7. All cross-system conversion is explicit, reversible where possible, and returns provenance.\n\n## 14. Proposed kotoba-lang modules\n\n```text\nkotoba-lang/resource-address\n  kotoba.resource.address     ; EDN model, normalize, validate\n  kotoba.resource.http        ; HTTP projection\n  kotoba.resource.resolve     ; resolver protocol and evidence model\n\nkotoba-lang/atproto\n  atproto.identifiers         ; DID, NSID, AT URI, rkey\n  atproto.repo                ; signed record/repository adapter\n\nkotoba-lang/multiformats\n  kotoba.cid                  ; CID/multibase/multicodec\n  kotoba.ipld                 ; canonical codec and Link adapter\n\ngftdcojp/net-kotobase\n  Datomic name/index and authorization adapter\n\ngftdcojp/net-kotobase-ipfs\n  IPFS/IPNS gateway and content-location adapter\n```\n\nNo module may implement a second private CID parser or AT URI grammar. KRP composes the specialized\nprotocol libraries through typed EDN.\n\n## 15. Adoption gate\n\nA repository adopting KRP provides `kotoba.edn` at its root and passes a common gate:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/protocol 1\n :kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n :kotoba/kind :game\n :kotoba/authority \"did:web:gftd.example\"\n :kotoba/routes {:root \"/gftd/nightglass\"\n                 :collections {:network.isekai/asset \"assets\"\n                               :network.isekai/fork \"forks\"}}\n :kotoba/records [{:collection :network.isekai/game :key \"self\"}]}\n```\n\nThe gate checks unique ownership, normalized slugs and qualified collections, route collisions,\nvalid DID/AT URI/CID syntax through their owning libraries, deterministic canonical encoding,\nlink closure, legacy aliases, and round-trip projections.\n\nThis protocol deliberately standardizes identity and resolution before standardizing UI. A game,\nmedical record, CAD model, payment order, dataset, or social post can share the same address algebra\nwithout sharing the same product routes or access policy.\n\n## 16. WebDAV / OCI Distribution / Nostr / SFTP projections (0.2 addendum, 2026-07-17)\n\nExtends §3's four identities and §10.1's mutable-head table to four additional wire protocols.\nNo fifth identity axis is introduced — every new protocol maps onto Name/Record/Content/Location\nas already defined.\n\n### 16.1 WebDAV\n\nWebDAV resources project onto **Name** (§3.1), same as HTTP/git paths — a WebDAV href is a\nstable, human-facing alias. Unlike flat S3-style keys, WebDAV requires distinguishing\ncollections (directories) from resources (files) as a first-class property of the stored\nrecord, not inferred from a trailing slash:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/repository \"gftd/nightglass\"\n :kotoba/collection :webdav/resource\n :kotoba/key \"assets/rifle.glb\"\n :webdav/collection? false}\n```\n\n`PROPFIND` walks Name-identity children under a given key prefix; it does not expose Content or\nRecord identity. WebDAV `LOCK` (out of scope for v0.1 implementations) would be additional\nmutable-name-authority state, not a new identity axis.\n\n### 16.2 OCI Distribution (and ORAS)\n\nAlready anticipated in §10.1's mutable-head table (\"OCI tag + digest ... WASM, GLB, CAR\ndistribution\") — this addendum confirms rather than changes that row. An OCI **tag** is a\nName-like mutable pointer; an OCI **digest** is Content (§3.3), a real CID-shaped hash even\nthough it is conventionally written `sha256:...` rather than a multibase CID string —\nimplementations MAY treat a digest as directly convertible to a CID (same hash function,\ndifferent textual encoding) rather than as a KRP-external checksum, unlike the ETag/fingerprint\ncarve-out in §3.3.\n\nORAS is not a distinct protocol for KRP purposes: it is a client convention over the same OCI\nDistribution API (manifests/blobs/referrers), so it projects identically — one Name (tag), one\nContent (digest) per artifact, no separate mapping needed.\n\n### 16.3 Nostr\n\nNostr event IDs and Blossom blob hashes are **Content** (§3.3), with the same carve-out §3.3\nalready establishes for S3 ETags and kotobase fingerprints: they are domain-specific content\nhashes (a Nostr event ID is SHA-256 of the event's canonical JSON serialization; a Blossom blob\nhash is a plain SHA-256 of the bytes), not general multibase CIDs, and MUST NOT be presented as\nCIDs without an explicit codec conversion:\n\n```clojure\n{:kotoba/collection :nostr/event\n :kotoba/key \"<32-byte-hex-event-id>\"\n :kotoba/content {:sha256 \"<32-byte-hex-event-id>\" :cid? false}}\n```\n\nA Nostr relay subscription (`REQ`/`EOSE`) is a live query over the Content collection, not a\nfifth identity — it has no independent KRP address of its own; results are ordinary Content\nentries.\n\n### 16.4 SFTP\n\nSFTP paths project onto **Location** only (§3.4) — a transport-specific place to retrieve\nmutable bytes, mirroring how git's dumb-HTTP surface is scoped in §8. SFTP does not introduce a\nName, Record, or Content identity of its own: a given file reachable over SFTP SHOULD already\nhave a KRP Name/Content identity through another projection (HTTP, git) if it needs one; SFTP is\nthe \"yet another transport\" case §3.4 exists for, not a new naming authority.\n\n## 17. Content URI has no path (0.3 addendum, 2026-08-14, ADR-2608145100)\n\n§3.3 Content identity is a hash. §2 `:kotoba/selector` is a walk inside that hash.\nThey must not be concatenated into a public URI.\n\nTaken from git / nix / holochain / unison (not UnixFS websites):\n\n- git blob SHA is identity; `HEAD:README.md` is a selector; the blob has its own SHA\n- nix store hash is identity; flake.lock names an output\n- holochain entry hash is identity; DNA/role is a local name\n- unison term hash is identity; names live in a namespace\n\nCanonical public content URIs:\n\n```\nipfs://{cidv1-base32}\nipns://{k51}\n```\n\n`:kotoba/selector` stays in the EDN address map. Hosts that walk an IPLD DAG name the\nleaf CID. `ipfs://{root}/a/b` is not a KRP content identity.\n\nHTTPS (`https://ipfs.kotobase.net/ipfs/{cid}`, `{cid}.ipfs.itonami.cloud`) is §3.4\nLocation / HTTP projection of the same CID, not a second identity. Gateway `/ipfs/{cid}`\nmeans \"get this block\", not \"walk a filesystem\".\n\nCIDv0 (`Qm…`) is not a public URI label. HTTP `/{org}/{repo}` remains §3.1 Name\n(discovery alias), not Content.\n"}]